// Practice quiz: Debugging with breakpoints
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/debugging-with-breakpoints

::o1 setting a breakpoint::How do you make a program pause at a line in IntelliJ IDEA? {
=Click the gutter next to the line number, then start the program with Debug#Correct. A breakpoint only pauses a program started with Debug, not with Run.
~Click the gutter, then start the program with Run#Not quite. Run ignores breakpoints, so the program does not pause.
~Type the word breakpoint in a comment on that line#Not quite. Comments are ignored by the compiler and the debugger.
~Add a println on that line#Not quite. A println shows a value but never pauses the program.
}

::o1 before or after::The debugger is paused on a breakpoint at line 9. Has line 9 run yet? {
=No: execution pauses before the breakpoint line runs#Correct. Stepping or resuming is what runs line 9.
~Yes: the pause happens after the line finishes#Not quite. A breakpoint pauses before its line runs.
~Half of it has run#Not quite. The debugger pauses between lines, not inside the middle of one.
~Only if line 9 calls a method#Not quite. Every breakpoint line pauses before it runs, whatever it contains.
}

::o2 entering a call::The debugger is paused on a line that calls helper(x), and you want to watch helper's own variables. Which control do you use? {
=Step Into#Correct. Step Into enters the method called on the current line and pauses at its first statement.
~Step Over#Not quite. Step Over runs the whole call and pauses at the next line of the current method.
~Step Out#Not quite. Step Out finishes the current method and returns to its caller.
~Resume#Not quite. Resume runs until the next breakpoint, or to the end if there is none.
}

::o2 leaving a method::You are paused inside a method and have seen enough of it. Which control finishes it and pauses back in the code that called it? {
=Step Out#Correct. Step Out runs the rest of the current method and pauses in its caller, unless another breakpoint is reached first.
~Step Into#Not quite. Step Into goes deeper into a call on the current line.
~Step Over#Not quite. Step Over moves one line at a time inside the current method.
~Resume#Not quite. Resume does not stop in the caller; it runs to the next breakpoint.
}

::o3 first wrong value::You expect x to be 2, 5 and then 9 after lines 3, 4 and 5 run. Stepping shows 2, 5 and then 7. On which line does x first take a wrong value? {
=Line 5#Correct. x matches your expectation after lines 3 and 4 and first differs after line 5.
~Line 3#Not quite. After line 3, x is 2, which is what you expected.
~Line 4#Not quite. After line 4, x is 5, which is still correct.
~The line that prints x#Not quite. The print only shows the value; it was already wrong after line 5.
}

::o3 after finding the line::You have found the line after which a variable first holds a wrong value. What should you look at next? {
=What that line reads, such as other variables or an index, to find the cause#Correct. The line where the value goes wrong points to the values it uses, which lead to the cause.
~Nothing more; delete that line#Not quite. Deleting it usually breaks something else; first find why it computes the wrong value.
~A breakpoint on every line of the program#Not quite. You already know where the value goes wrong, so more breakpoints add noise.
~The output after restarting with Run#Not quite. Run does not pause, so it shows only the final wrong result again.
}
